            <ArticleTitle>The Mini-Mental State Exam (MMSE) is not sensitive to cognitive impairment in bariatric surgery candidates.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>553-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.soard.2013.09.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1550-7289(13)00305-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cognitive dysfunction is common among bariatric surgery candidates and associated with poorer weight loss outcomes. Identification of a brief screening measure to detect cognitive impairment in this population is needed, because comprehensive neuropsychological evaluations may not be available in all clinical settings.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The present study examined the utility of the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) for detecting cognitive impairment in 30 bariatric surgery candidates by comparing impairment on the MMSE (at varying cutoffs) to impairment on a comprehensive neuropsychological test battery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Results indicated that the MMSE showed low sensitivity and specificity in identifying impairment, even at the more stringent MMSE cutoffs of 27 and 28.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These findings suggest that the MMSE is a poor screener for cognitive impairment in bariatric surgery candidates. Future research is needed to identify or develop cognitive screeners for use in this population.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2013 Published by American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery on behalf of American Society for Bariatric Surgery.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
